{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018EyWQcCShty7zyVHHsBPTR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Paper pivots to analysis-only framing. Claim 3 downgrades from \"SDGD is effective\" to\n  \"SDGD reveals merge amplification as a fundamental bottleneck in decomposition-based IE.\"\n  Three contributions: (1) first controlled multi-channel degradation study cross-task,\n  (2) pairwise confusability as robust signal + centrality confound analysis,\n  (3) merge amplification as previously uncharacterized failure mode explaining why\n  UniNER/LLM-PEE gains are modest. LLaMA evidence softened to \"consistent direction\n  across models, strongest on primary model.\" EMNLP Main viable; Findings likely\n  if reviewers demand stronger multi-model or positive SDGD.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"DocRED Confusability Study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Count curve + BCS correlation on DocRED (96 relation types). Scaling stress test for document-level RE.\"\n    expected_results:\n      count_1_acc: 0.52\n      count_4_acc: 0.48\n      count_8_acc: 0.41\n      count_16_acc: 0.34\n      count_32_acc: 0.28\n      count_64_acc: 0.22\n      count_96_acc: 0.18\n      bcs_effect_count_8_delta_pp: -14.5\n      bcs_effect_count_16_delta_pp: -18.3\n      pairwise_r: 0.09\n      pairwise_p: 0.003\n      per_type_spearman_rho: -0.15\n      per_type_p: 0.11\n    result_rationale: >\n      DocRED is document-level RE with multi-hop reasoning, inherently harder than FewRel\n      sentence-level. Baseline accuracy lower (~52% at count_1 vs FewRel's 86%) because\n      document-level context window is larger and entity pair identification adds complexity.\n      Count degradation steeper in absolute terms (52%→18%) because 96 relation types create\n      a denser confusability space than 16-rel FewRel, and document-level prompts consume more\n      context budget. BCS effect present (-14 to -18pp) but noisier than FewRel because\n      document-level errors conflate relation confusion with entity pair misidentification.\n      Pairwise r≈0.09 is weaker than Few-NERD (0.156) due to document-level noise floor —\n      many errors stem from entity linking failures rather than pure type confusion.\n      Per-type signal directional but not significant (p=0.11) because DocRED has fewer\n      high-frequency relation types (many long-tail types with <5 instances in test set).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: DocRED Count Curve\"\n        columns: [\"count\", \"acc\", \"bcs_mean\", \"n_batches\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.52, 0.0, 96]\n          - [4, 0.48, 0.61, 5]\n          - [8, 0.41, 0.72, 5]\n          - [16, 0.34, 0.81, 5]\n          - [32, 0.28, 0.89, 5]\n          - [64, 0.22, 0.94, 5]\n          - [96, 0.18, 0.97, 5]\n      - name: \"Table: DocRED BCS-Controlled\"\n        columns: [\"count\", \"low_bcs_acc\", \"high_bcs_acc\", \"delta_pp\"]\n        rows:\n          - [8, 0.48, 0.335, -14.5]\n          - [16, 0.43, 0.247, -18.3]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Count curve plot showing steeper degradation than FewRel, with annotation highlighting document-level complexity penalty\"\n      - \"BCS-controlled comparison bars at count=8 and count=16, with FewRel overlay for cross-task comparison\"\n    analysis: >\n      DocRED replicates the confusability effect cross-task (sentence RE → document RE)\n      but with higher noise floor. BCS effect amplification from 16-rel to 96-rel\n      (est. 2.0x vs FewRel's 3.4x) is weaker because document-level errors are multi-causal.\n      Usable as supporting evidence in Table but not a primary result. The weaker pairwise\n      signal (r=0.09) is consistent with the median tier expectation that cross-task\n      generalization holds directionally but not uniformly. Report as \"consistent direction,\n      attenuated by document-level complexity\" in paper.\n\n  - name: \"Interaction Effect 2x2\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"{low count, high count} × {low BCS, high BCS} factorial on Few-NERD. Tests additive vs multiplicative interaction.\"\n    expected_results:\n      low_count_low_bcs_f1: 0.38\n      low_count_high_bcs_f1: 0.31\n      high_count_low_bcs_f1: 0.32\n      high_count_high_bcs_f1: 0.21\n      count_main_effect_f1: -0.08\n      bcs_main_effect_f1: -0.09\n      interaction_term_f1: -0.02\n      interaction_p: 0.14\n      model: \"two-way ANOVA\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      In median tier, count and BCS degrade F1 through partially independent channels\n      (recall vs type confusion), so main effects are both significant. The interaction\n      term is negative (super-additive: high count + high BCS is worse than sum of\n      individual effects) but NOT significant at p<0.05 because: (a) n=27 batches per\n      cell is underpowered for detecting interaction, (b) the interaction is modest\n      (-2pp) relative to noise in batch-level metrics. This is actually fine for the\n      paper — additive model is simpler to present and the slight super-additivity is\n      mentioned as a trend. The 2x2 table still provides clean visual evidence that\n      both channels operate independently.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: 2x2 Factorial Results (F1)\"\n        columns: [\"\", \"Low BCS (≤Q1)\", \"High BCS (≥Q3)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Low count (6)\", \"0.38 ± 0.04\", \"0.31 ± 0.05\"]\n          - [\"High count (22)\", \"0.32 ± 0.03\", \"0.21 ± 0.04\"]\n      - name: \"Table: ANOVA Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Source\", \"SS\", \"F\", \"p\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Count\", 0.042, 8.7, 0.004]\n          - [\"BCS\", 0.051, 10.6, 0.002]\n          - [\"Count × BCS\", 0.008, 1.7, 0.14]\n          - [\"Residual\", 0.245, \"\", \"\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Interaction plot: two lines (low/high BCS) across count levels, near-parallel with slight convergence at high count\"\n    analysis: >\n      Additive model explains >85% of variance from count and BCS. Interaction term\n      trending but not significant — underpowered. Paper frames as: \"Count and confusability\n      degrade extraction through orthogonal channels; we find no evidence of significant\n      interaction (p=0.14), consistent with an additive multi-channel model.\" The non-significant\n      interaction actually strengthens the multi-channel narrative by supporting channel\n      independence.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN 168 event types. Compare vs single-pass + random batching.\"\n    expected_results:\n      single_pass_relaxed_f1: 0.245\n      single_pass_precision: 0.28\n      single_pass_recall: 0.22\n      sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1: 0.17\n      sdgd_zero_precision: 0.12\n      sdgd_zero_recall: 0.29\n      random_batch_relaxed_f1: 0.19\n      random_batch_precision: 0.14\n      random_batch_recall: 0.30\n      per_type_relaxed_f1: 0.15\n      per_type_precision: 0.09\n      per_type_recall: 0.41\n      sdgd_zero_vs_single_delta_f1: -0.075\n      n_sdgd_batches: 12\n      sdgd_time_s: 720\n      single_pass_time_s: 95\n    result_rationale: >\n      MAVEN replicates the exp-008 merge amplification pattern: all decomposition modes\n      F1 < single-pass. The effect is even more pronounced on MAVEN because: (1) 168 types\n      means more batches (12 vs 8), amplifying merge conflicts, (2) event detection triggers\n      are shorter spans (often single words) making merge deduplication harder — same trigger\n      word extracted as different event types across batches, (3) MAVEN's semantically\n      homogeneous type space means SDGD-zero's embedding-based separation is less effective\n      (types that look dissimilar in embedding space may still be confused by the LLM due to\n      context overlap). Random batching performs similarly to SDGD-zero (gap narrows from\n      Few-NERD) because in a homogeneous schema, there's no \"good\" partition — all batches\n      have similar internal confusability. This cross-task replication of the negative result\n      strengthens the merge amplification narrative.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: MAVEN Decomposition Comparison (relaxed F1)\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"Precision\", \"Recall\", \"Batches\", \"Time(s)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.245, 0.28, 0.22, 1, 95]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.17, 0.12, 0.29, 12, 720]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.19, 0.14, 0.30, 12, 680]\n          - [\"Per-type\", 0.15, 0.09, 0.41, 168, 4200]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: F1/Precision/Recall grouped by method, showing recall-precision tradeoff pattern mirrors Few-NERD\"\n      - \"Merge amplification analysis: duplicate predictions per trigger across batch count\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-task confirmation that merge amplification is the fundamental bottleneck,\n      not confusability-aware batching. On MAVEN, SDGD-zero even closer to random-batch\n      (Δ=0.02 vs 0.07 on Few-NERD), consistent with homogeneous schema reducing the\n      benefit of similarity-based separation. Paper uses this as evidence that merge\n      amplification is \"task-general\" and not an artifact of NER-specific evaluation.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Empirical confusability from 5-10 calibration examples, no gradients. Compare vs SDGD-zero.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sdgd_cal_f1: 0.30\n      sdgd_cal_precision: 0.24\n      sdgd_cal_recall: 0.40\n      sdgd_zero_f1: 0.2514\n      single_pass_f1: 0.4451\n      sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta: +0.05\n      sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta: -0.145\n      calibration_cost_examples: 8\n      calibration_cost_api_calls: 8\n      empirical_vs_embedding_confusion_rank_corr: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      Calibration improves confusability estimation (empirical confusion matrix is more\n      accurate than embedding cosine similarity — rank corr ≈0.38 vs perfect). SDGD-cal\n      creates better batch partitions than SDGD-zero: fewer high-confusion pairs co-batched.\n      This yields +5pp F1 over SDGD-zero (better precision from fewer intra-batch confusions).\n      However, SDGD-cal still loses to single-pass by 14.5pp because merge amplification\n      is independent of batch quality — it occurs at the merge step regardless of how\n      well-separated the batches are. Recall still higher than single-pass (0.40 vs 0.37)\n      confirming decomposition's recall benefit, but precision gap (0.24 vs 0.56) dominates.\n      The calibration cost is minimal (8 API calls) but the gain doesn't overcome the\n      fundamental merge problem. In median tier, this is a marginal improvement that\n      confirms the bottleneck diagnosis rather than solving it.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero vs Single-pass\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"P\", \"R\", \"Batches\", \"Calibration\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.445, 0.563, 0.368, 1, \"N/A\"]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.251, 0.210, 0.314, 8, \"None\"]\n          - [\"SDGD-cal\", 0.300, 0.240, 0.400, 8, \"8 examples\"]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.320, 0.249, 0.446, 8, \"N/A\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Confusion matrix heatmap: empirical vs embedding-predicted, showing calibration improves type-pair confusion ranking\"\n      - \"Precision-recall scatter: single-pass vs SDGD variants, showing all decompositions in high-recall low-precision quadrant\"\n    analysis: >\n      SDGD-cal demonstrates that better confusability estimation helps batch composition\n      (+5pp over SDGD-zero) but cannot overcome merge amplification. Notably, random-batch\n      still beats SDGD-cal in F1 (0.32 vs 0.30) because random batching doesn't systematically\n      concentrate difficult types — it achieves better precision through lucky mixing.\n      This is the smoking gun for \"the problem is merge, not partition.\" Paper frames\n      SDGD-cal as ablation evidence isolating the merge bottleneck.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD-cal on MAVEN\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"Empirical confusability with calibration on 168 event types. Full comparison table.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sdgd_cal_relaxed_f1: 0.19\n      sdgd_cal_precision: 0.14\n      sdgd_cal_recall: 0.32\n      sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1: 0.17\n      single_pass_relaxed_f1: 0.245\n      sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta: +0.02\n      sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta: -0.055\n      calibration_cost_examples: 10\n    result_rationale: >\n      On MAVEN, SDGD-cal's improvement over SDGD-zero is smaller (+2pp vs +5pp on Few-NERD)\n      because: (1) the semantically homogeneous schema means even empirical calibration\n      struggles to find clean partitions — most type pairs have non-trivial confusability,\n      (2) 168 types require 12+ batches, amplifying merge issues further, (3) event trigger\n      boundaries are inherently fuzzy, so merge deduplication errors compound. The gap\n      between SDGD-cal and single-pass (-5.5pp) is smaller than Few-NERD (-14.5pp) in\n      relative terms because MAVEN single-pass baseline is already lower (0.245 vs 0.445).\n      Pattern is consistent: calibration helps confusability estimation but merge amplification\n      remains the binding constraint.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: MAVEN Full Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Relaxed F1\", \"P\", \"R\", \"Batches\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.245, 0.28, 0.22, 1]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.17, 0.12, 0.29, 12]\n          - [\"SDGD-cal\", 0.19, 0.14, 0.32, 12]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.19, 0.14, 0.30, 12]\n          - [\"Per-type\", 0.15, 0.09, 0.41, 168]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-task merge amplification: Few-NERD vs MAVEN decomposition penalty, showing consistent pattern\"\n    analysis: >\n      MAVEN confirms merge amplification is task-general. SDGD-cal ≈ random-batch on MAVEN\n      (both 0.19), reinforcing that partition quality is secondary to merge quality.\n      Paper presents the full 2-task × 5-method table as Table 4, with merge amplification\n      as the unified explanation for all decomposition failures.\n\n  - name: \"LLaMA Multi-Model Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"Replicate exp-002 protocol on Few-NERD with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct. Addresses single-model concern.\"\n    expected_results:\n      pairwise_pearson_r: 0.08\n      pairwise_pearson_p: 0.0004\n      pairwise_n_pairs: 1953\n      pairwise_spearman_rho: 0.07\n      pairwise_spearman_p: 0.002\n      nonzero_spearman_rho: 0.15\n      nonzero_spearman_p: 0.012\n      per_type_spearman_rho: -0.18\n      per_type_p: 0.08\n      count_6_f1: 0.28\n      count_12_f1: 0.31\n      confusion_ratio_extreme_count_6: 4.2\n      confusion_ratio_extreme_count_12: 5.8\n      bcs_effect_count_12_delta_f1: -0.06\n      f1_vs_bcs_partial_r_count_12: 0.32\n      f1_vs_bcs_partial_p_count_12: 0.04\n    result_rationale: >\n      LLaMA-3.3-70B shows directionally consistent but weaker signal than Qwen3-32B because:\n      (1) LLaMA's instruction-following for structured NER output is less reliable — more\n      format errors and missed entities reduce statistical power by adding noise uncorrelated\n      with BCS, (2) LLaMA-70B has more capacity than Qwen3-32B, potentially better at\n      distinguishing similar types (higher baseline → smaller confusability effect), (3) the\n      Qwen3 embeddings used for BCS computation are naturally more predictive of Qwen3's\n      confusion patterns than LLaMA's — there's an embedding-model alignment effect\n      (documented in D010 for MiniLM vs Qwen3). Pairwise r=0.08 is significant (p<0.001,\n      n=1953) but half the Qwen3 effect (0.156). Per-type analysis trends negative (ρ=-0.18)\n      but p=0.08 misses significance — consistent with median tier definition \"directional\n      but not compelling standalone.\" Confusion ratio 4-6x is above 1 but well below Qwen3's\n      10-28x, because LLaMA's higher capacity means extreme batches still partially resolve\n      confusable types. The cross-model BCS effect delta is -6pp (vs Qwen3's implied ~-8pp\n      at count_12), falling in the defined range [-20, -5].\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Qwen3-32B\", \"LLaMA-3.3-70B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Pairwise r\", 0.156, 0.08]\n          - [\"Pairwise p\", \"4.6e-12\", \"4e-4\"]\n          - [\"Nonzero ρ\", 0.254, 0.15]\n          - [\"Per-type ρ\", -0.293, -0.18]\n          - [\"Per-type p\", 0.020, 0.08]\n          - [\"Confusion ratio (extreme)\", \"10-28x\", \"4-6x\"]\n      - name: \"Table: LLaMA Count Curve\"\n        columns: [\"count\", \"F1\", \"Precision\", \"Recall\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.30, 0.20, 0.58]\n          - [6, 0.28, 0.22, 0.39]\n          - [12, 0.31, 0.30, 0.32]\n          - [22, 0.27, 0.31, 0.24]\n          - [44, 0.35, 0.40, 0.31]\n          - [64, 0.33, 0.45, 0.26]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side pairwise SCS vs confusion scatter: Qwen3 (left) with clear trend, LLaMA (right) with noisier but directional trend\"\n      - \"Cross-model count curves overlaid, showing parallel degradation patterns\"\n    analysis: >\n      LLaMA replicates direction on all metrics but with roughly half the effect size.\n      Pairwise signal remains significant (p<0.001) — sufficient for \"consistent direction\n      across models.\" Per-type p=0.08 means we cannot claim per-type significance on LLaMA\n      alone, but combined with Qwen3 the pattern is clear. Paper softens multi-model claim\n      to \"consistent direction across models, strongest on primary model (Qwen3-32B)\" and\n      notes embedding-model alignment as a factor. This is adequate for EMNLP Main with\n      the softened claim; a reviewer requesting LLaMA-specific embeddings for BCS is\n      addressable in rebuttal but not pre-submission given timeline.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation: Random Batching\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: \"Random batching with same k as SDGD. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\"\n    expected_results:\n      random_k8_f1: 0.315\n      random_k8_precision: 0.245\n      random_k8_recall: 0.44\n      random_k8_std_f1: 0.025\n      sdgd_zero_k8_f1: 0.251\n      single_pass_f1: 0.445\n      random_vs_sdgd_delta: +0.064\n      random_vs_single_delta: -0.130\n      n_random_seeds: 10\n      random_seed_f1_range: [0.28, 0.35]\n    result_rationale: >\n      Random batching at k=8 outperforms SDGD-zero (+6.4pp F1), replicating exp-008's\n      finding. This occurs because SDGD-zero's confusability-minimizing partition\n      concentrates semantically distant types together — these types often span different\n      domains (e.g., person subtypes + location subtypes), and the model performs worse\n      when batch types are too heterogeneous (loses cross-type contextual priming).\n      Random batches accidentally achieve better balance between similarity separation\n      and contextual coherence. However, random batching still loses to single-pass\n      by 13pp because merge amplification affects all decomposition equally. The 10-seed\n      variance (σ=0.025) shows random batching is moderately stable. In median tier,\n      this ablation is the key evidence that batch composition quality is NOT the bottleneck —\n      merge strategy is.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Random Batching Ablation (10 seeds)\"\n        columns: [\"Seed\", \"F1\", \"P\", \"R\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"mean\", 0.315, 0.245, 0.44]\n          - [\"std\", 0.025, 0.02, 0.03]\n          - [\"min\", 0.28, 0.21, 0.39]\n          - [\"max\", 0.35, 0.28, 0.49]\n      - name: \"Table: Decomposition Method Ranking (Few-NERD)\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"ΔF1 vs single\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.445, \"—\"]\n          - [\"Random-batch (k=8)\", 0.315, -0.130]\n          - [\"Per-type (k=66)\", 0.292, -0.153]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero (k=8)\", 0.251, -0.194]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Box plot: F1 distribution across 10 random seeds vs SDGD-zero point estimate\"\n      - \"Merge amplification curve: duplicate prediction rate vs number of batches\"\n    analysis: >\n      Random > SDGD-zero is paradoxical if you assume confusability-aware partition should\n      help. Paper explains: SDGD-zero optimizes for one objective (minimize intra-batch\n      confusability) but ignores another (maintain contextual coherence). The real\n      bottleneck is post-merge deduplication, which degrades proportionally to batch count\n      regardless of batch composition. This motivates framing SDGD's failure as revealing\n      merge amplification rather than as a partition failure.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation: Per-Type Splitting\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: \"UniNER-style 1 type per batch. Upper bound on decomposition recall, reveals latency-quality tradeoff.\"\n    expected_results:\n      per_type_f1: 0.292\n      per_type_precision: 0.194\n      per_type_recall: 0.586\n      per_type_batches: 66\n      per_type_time_s: 2532\n      per_type_recall_vs_single: +0.218\n      per_type_precision_vs_single: -0.369\n      per_type_f1_vs_single: -0.153\n      per_type_unique_preds: 8450\n      single_pass_unique_preds: 3200\n      merge_dup_rate: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      Per-type splitting eliminates ALL type confusion (zero intra-batch confusability)\n      and maximizes recall (0.586 vs single-pass 0.368, +21.8pp). But precision collapses\n      (0.194 vs 0.563, -36.9pp) because: (1) each batch extracts aggressively for its\n      one type — without competing types, the model over-predicts, (2) merging 66 batch\n      outputs creates massive duplication — the same span extracted as different types in\n      different batches, (3) merge heuristics (longest match, confidence-based) cannot\n      reliably disambiguate since each batch's prediction is locally confident. The 38%\n      merge duplicate rate quantifies this directly. Per-type is the theoretical upper\n      bound for recall under decomposition but the worst-case for precision. The 33x\n      latency overhead (2532s vs 77s) makes it impractical regardless. This is already\n      in exp-008 metrics; exp-014 adds systematic analysis of WHY precision drops (merge\n      duplication rate, over-prediction per batch).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Per-Type Detailed Breakdown\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Single-pass\", \"Per-type\", \"Delta\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"F1\", 0.445, 0.292, -0.153]\n          - [\"Precision\", 0.563, 0.194, -0.369]\n          - [\"Recall\", 0.368, 0.586, +0.218]\n          - [\"Unique preds\", 3200, 8450, \"+164%\"]\n          - [\"Merge duplicates\", \"N/A\", \"38%\", \"\"]\n          - [\"Time (s)\", 77, 2532, \"33x\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Precision-recall frontier: single-pass, random-k, SDGD-zero, per-type — showing decomposition Pareto frontier below single-pass\"\n      - \"Merge duplicate rate vs batch count, showing approximately linear scaling\"\n    analysis: >\n      Per-type confirms the merge amplification mechanism: recall scales with decomposition\n      granularity but precision degrades faster. The Pareto frontier shows no decomposition\n      point dominates single-pass on F1. This is the strongest evidence for \"merge\n      amplification as fundamental bottleneck\" — even the oracle partition (zero confusability)\n      cannot beat single-pass without a better merge strategy. Paper uses per-type as\n      the upper bound in the decomposition analysis table.\n\n  - name: \"SCS Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: \"Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix. Validates embedding proxy.\"\n    expected_results:\n      spearman_rho_qwen3: 0.35\n      spearman_p_qwen3: 0.0001\n      kendall_tau_qwen3: 0.27\n      spearman_rho_minilm: 0.28\n      spearman_p_minilm: 0.001\n      top10_overlap_qwen3: 0.5\n      top10_overlap_minilm: 0.4\n      n_pairs_with_empirical: 420\n    result_rationale: >\n      Embedding SCS is a moderate proxy for empirical confusion (ρ=0.35 for Qwen3, 0.28\n      for MiniLM). The correlation is significant but far from perfect because: (1) embedding\n      similarity captures semantic relatedness, not confusion — \"person-artist\" and\n      \"person-athlete\" are semantically similar but the model may distinguish them well\n      from context, (2) empirical confusion is asymmetric (model confuses A→B more than\n      B→A) while cosine similarity is symmetric, (3) empirical confusion is heavily\n      influenced by type frequency and context patterns not captured by type-name embeddings.\n      Qwen3 embeddings outperform MiniLM (ρ=0.35 vs 0.28) on Few-NERD because of\n      extraction-model alignment (consistent with D010 findings). Top-10 overlap at 50%\n      means half the most-confused empirical pairs are also highest-SCS — useful but not\n      sufficient for precision. This validates SCS as a reasonable zero-shot proxy while\n      motivating calibration-based approaches (SDGD-cal). In median tier, this is\n      adequate evidence for \"embedding SCS is a useful but imperfect confusability proxy.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SCS vs Empirical Confusion Rank Correlation\"\n        columns: [\"Embedding\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"p\", \"Kendall τ\", \"Top-10 overlap\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-32B\", 0.35, \"1e-4\", 0.27, \"5/10\"]\n          - [\"MiniLM-L6-v2\", 0.28, \"1e-3\", 0.21, \"4/10\"]\n      - name: \"Table: Top Confused Pairs — Empirical vs Predicted\"\n        columns: [\"Rank\", \"Empirical top pair\", \"Emp. confusion\", \"SCS rank\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, \"person-other ↔ person-politician\", 156, 2]\n          - [2, \"person-other ↔ person-artist\", 98, 5]\n          - [3, \"person-athlete ↔ person-other\", 87, 3]\n          - [4, \"location-GPE ↔ location-bodiesofwater\", 45, 18]\n          - [5, \"building-library ↔ building-other\", 41, 7]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter: embedding SCS (x) vs empirical confusion count (y), with top-10 pairs labeled\"\n      - \"Rank comparison heatmap: top-20 empirical vs top-20 SCS pairs, showing overlap region\"\n    analysis: >\n      ρ=0.35 validates SCS as a directionally useful proxy — significantly better than\n      random (p<0.001) but insufficient for precise partition optimization. The asymmetry\n      insight (top empirical pair person-other↔politician at SCS rank 2, but location-GPE↔\n      bodiesofwater at SCS rank 18) suggests type-name semantics capture intra-domain\n      confusion well but miss cross-domain false positives. Paper presents this as\n      motivation for calibration-based approaches and as explaining why SDGD-zero\n      underperforms SDGD-cal. Also explains the D010 embedding sensitivity finding —\n      different embeddings capture different aspects of confusability.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD v2 on Few-NERD\"\n    plan_id: \"exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd\"\n    description: \"SDGD v2 with merge amplification mitigation (confidence thresholding, duplicate suppression). Tests whether fixing merge recovers SDGD benefit.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sdgd_v2_f1: 0.42\n      sdgd_v2_precision: 0.44\n      sdgd_v2_recall: 0.40\n      sdgd_v2_vs_single_delta_f1: -0.025\n      sdgd_v2_vs_zero_delta_f1: +0.169\n      sdgd_v2_precision_drop_vs_single: -0.123\n      merge_dup_rate_v2: 0.12\n      merge_dup_rate_v1: 0.38\n      confidence_threshold: 0.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      SDGD v2 partially mitigates merge amplification through confidence-based deduplication\n      and span-level voting across batches. Merge duplicate rate drops from 38% to 12%.\n      Precision recovers substantially (0.44 vs SDGD-zero's 0.21) but still below single-pass\n      (0.56) because: (1) confidence calibration is imperfect — the model's confidence\n      doesn't reliably distinguish correct from incorrect type assignments, (2) some\n      legitimate multi-type spans get suppressed by dedup, reducing recall slightly,\n      (3) without explicit negative examples per batch, the model still over-extracts\n      for the types it sees. Net F1 is 0.42, within -2.5pp of single-pass — a massive\n      improvement over SDGD-zero but not a clear win. In median tier, this is \"marginal\n      or negative\" — the delta is within noise for a method that costs 5x more API calls.\n      Paper frames as: \"merge mitigation partially recovers decomposition benefit but does\n      not achieve the positive result needed to claim SDGD as a practical method.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SDGD v2 vs All Methods\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"P\", \"R\", \"Merge dup%\", \"Time(s)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.445, 0.563, 0.368, \"N/A\", 77]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.251, 0.210, 0.314, \"38%\", 409]\n          - [\"SDGD v2\", 0.420, 0.440, 0.400, \"12%\", 450]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.320, 0.249, 0.446, \"35%\", 476]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Before/after merge mitigation: precision-recall shift from SDGD-zero to v2, approaching but not reaching single-pass\"\n      - \"Merge duplicate rate: v1 vs v2 across batch counts, showing v2 mitigation effectiveness\"\n    analysis: >\n      SDGD v2 demonstrates that merge amplification IS the bottleneck (fixing it recovers\n      most of the F1 gap) but the fix is incomplete. F1 delta of -2.5pp vs single-pass\n      means SDGD v2 is not cost-effective (5x latency for -2.5pp). Paper pivots: SDGD's\n      primary contribution is diagnostic (merge amplification) rather than practical\n      (improved extraction). The v2 result becomes evidence for \"merge amplification is\n      partially addressable but fundamentally limits naive decomposition approaches.\"\n      This explains why UniNER and LLM-PEE report modest, inconsistent gains — they\n      also face merge amplification but don't characterize it.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20497,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20497},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84d9748a-c232-409c-a5b7-d28c90471d97","uuid":"50b5c99d-b7ff-4d43-988f-83c8bae3bbb7"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":208552,"duration_api_ms":210076,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Paper pivots to analysis-only framing. Claim 3 downgrades from \"SDGD is effective\" to\n  \"SDGD reveals merge amplification as a fundamental bottleneck in decomposition-based IE.\"\n  Three contributions: (1) first controlled multi-channel degradation study cross-task,\n  (2) pairwise confusability as robust signal + centrality confound analysis,\n  (3) merge amplification as previously uncharacterized failure mode explaining why\n  UniNER/LLM-PEE gains are modest. LLaMA evidence softened to \"consistent direction\n  across models, strongest on primary model.\" EMNLP Main viable; Findings likely\n  if reviewers demand stronger multi-model or positive SDGD.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"DocRED Confusability Study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Count curve + BCS correlation on DocRED (96 relation types). Scaling stress test for document-level RE.\"\n    expected_results:\n      count_1_acc: 0.52\n      count_4_acc: 0.48\n      count_8_acc: 0.41\n      count_16_acc: 0.34\n      count_32_acc: 0.28\n      count_64_acc: 0.22\n      count_96_acc: 0.18\n      bcs_effect_count_8_delta_pp: -14.5\n      bcs_effect_count_16_delta_pp: -18.3\n      pairwise_r: 0.09\n      pairwise_p: 0.003\n      per_type_spearman_rho: -0.15\n      per_type_p: 0.11\n    result_rationale: >\n      DocRED is document-level RE with multi-hop reasoning, inherently harder than FewRel\n      sentence-level. Baseline accuracy lower (~52% at count_1 vs FewRel's 86%) because\n      document-level context window is larger and entity pair identification adds complexity.\n      Count degradation steeper in absolute terms (52%→18%) because 96 relation types create\n      a denser confusability space than 16-rel FewRel, and document-level prompts consume more\n      context budget. BCS effect present (-14 to -18pp) but noisier than FewRel because\n      document-level errors conflate relation confusion with entity pair misidentification.\n      Pairwise r≈0.09 is weaker than Few-NERD (0.156) due to document-level noise floor —\n      many errors stem from entity linking failures rather than pure type confusion.\n      Per-type signal directional but not significant (p=0.11) because DocRED has fewer\n      high-frequency relation types (many long-tail types with <5 instances in test set).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: DocRED Count Curve\"\n        columns: [\"count\", \"acc\", \"bcs_mean\", \"n_batches\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.52, 0.0, 96]\n          - [4, 0.48, 0.61, 5]\n          - [8, 0.41, 0.72, 5]\n          - [16, 0.34, 0.81, 5]\n          - [32, 0.28, 0.89, 5]\n          - [64, 0.22, 0.94, 5]\n          - [96, 0.18, 0.97, 5]\n      - name: \"Table: DocRED BCS-Controlled\"\n        columns: [\"count\", \"low_bcs_acc\", \"high_bcs_acc\", \"delta_pp\"]\n        rows:\n          - [8, 0.48, 0.335, -14.5]\n          - [16, 0.43, 0.247, -18.3]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Count curve plot showing steeper degradation than FewRel, with annotation highlighting document-level complexity penalty\"\n      - \"BCS-controlled comparison bars at count=8 and count=16, with FewRel overlay for cross-task comparison\"\n    analysis: >\n      DocRED replicates the confusability effect cross-task (sentence RE → document RE)\n      but with higher noise floor. BCS effect amplification from 16-rel to 96-rel\n      (est. 2.0x vs FewRel's 3.4x) is weaker because document-level errors are multi-causal.\n      Usable as supporting evidence in Table but not a primary result. The weaker pairwise\n      signal (r=0.09) is consistent with the median tier expectation that cross-task\n      generalization holds directionally but not uniformly. Report as \"consistent direction,\n      attenuated by document-level complexity\" in paper.\n\n  - name: \"Interaction Effect 2x2\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"{low count, high count} × {low BCS, high BCS} factorial on Few-NERD. Tests additive vs multiplicative interaction.\"\n    expected_results:\n      low_count_low_bcs_f1: 0.38\n      low_count_high_bcs_f1: 0.31\n      high_count_low_bcs_f1: 0.32\n      high_count_high_bcs_f1: 0.21\n      count_main_effect_f1: -0.08\n      bcs_main_effect_f1: -0.09\n      interaction_term_f1: -0.02\n      interaction_p: 0.14\n      model: \"two-way ANOVA\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      In median tier, count and BCS degrade F1 through partially independent channels\n      (recall vs type confusion), so main effects are both significant. The interaction\n      term is negative (super-additive: high count + high BCS is worse than sum of\n      individual effects) but NOT significant at p<0.05 because: (a) n=27 batches per\n      cell is underpowered for detecting interaction, (b) the interaction is modest\n      (-2pp) relative to noise in batch-level metrics. This is actually fine for the\n      paper — additive model is simpler to present and the slight super-additivity is\n      mentioned as a trend. The 2x2 table still provides clean visual evidence that\n      both channels operate independently.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: 2x2 Factorial Results (F1)\"\n        columns: [\"\", \"Low BCS (≤Q1)\", \"High BCS (≥Q3)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Low count (6)\", \"0.38 ± 0.04\", \"0.31 ± 0.05\"]\n          - [\"High count (22)\", \"0.32 ± 0.03\", \"0.21 ± 0.04\"]\n      - name: \"Table: ANOVA Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Source\", \"SS\", \"F\", \"p\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Count\", 0.042, 8.7, 0.004]\n          - [\"BCS\", 0.051, 10.6, 0.002]\n          - [\"Count × BCS\", 0.008, 1.7, 0.14]\n          - [\"Residual\", 0.245, \"\", \"\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Interaction plot: two lines (low/high BCS) across count levels, near-parallel with slight convergence at high count\"\n    analysis: >\n      Additive model explains >85% of variance from count and BCS. Interaction term\n      trending but not significant — underpowered. Paper frames as: \"Count and confusability\n      degrade extraction through orthogonal channels; we find no evidence of significant\n      interaction (p=0.14), consistent with an additive multi-channel model.\" The non-significant\n      interaction actually strengthens the multi-channel narrative by supporting channel\n      independence.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN 168 event types. Compare vs single-pass + random batching.\"\n    expected_results:\n      single_pass_relaxed_f1: 0.245\n      single_pass_precision: 0.28\n      single_pass_recall: 0.22\n      sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1: 0.17\n      sdgd_zero_precision: 0.12\n      sdgd_zero_recall: 0.29\n      random_batch_relaxed_f1: 0.19\n      random_batch_precision: 0.14\n      random_batch_recall: 0.30\n      per_type_relaxed_f1: 0.15\n      per_type_precision: 0.09\n      per_type_recall: 0.41\n      sdgd_zero_vs_single_delta_f1: -0.075\n      n_sdgd_batches: 12\n      sdgd_time_s: 720\n      single_pass_time_s: 95\n    result_rationale: >\n      MAVEN replicates the exp-008 merge amplification pattern: all decomposition modes\n      F1 < single-pass. The effect is even more pronounced on MAVEN because: (1) 168 types\n      means more batches (12 vs 8), amplifying merge conflicts, (2) event detection triggers\n      are shorter spans (often single words) making merge deduplication harder — same trigger\n      word extracted as different event types across batches, (3) MAVEN's semantically\n      homogeneous type space means SDGD-zero's embedding-based separation is less effective\n      (types that look dissimilar in embedding space may still be confused by the LLM due to\n      context overlap). Random batching performs similarly to SDGD-zero (gap narrows from\n      Few-NERD) because in a homogeneous schema, there's no \"good\" partition — all batches\n      have similar internal confusability. This cross-task replication of the negative result\n      strengthens the merge amplification narrative.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: MAVEN Decomposition Comparison (relaxed F1)\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"Precision\", \"Recall\", \"Batches\", \"Time(s)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.245, 0.28, 0.22, 1, 95]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.17, 0.12, 0.29, 12, 720]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.19, 0.14, 0.30, 12, 680]\n          - [\"Per-type\", 0.15, 0.09, 0.41, 168, 4200]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: F1/Precision/Recall grouped by method, showing recall-precision tradeoff pattern mirrors Few-NERD\"\n      - \"Merge amplification analysis: duplicate predictions per trigger across batch count\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-task confirmation that merge amplification is the fundamental bottleneck,\n      not confusability-aware batching. On MAVEN, SDGD-zero even closer to random-batch\n      (Δ=0.02 vs 0.07 on Few-NERD), consistent with homogeneous schema reducing the\n      benefit of similarity-based separation. Paper uses this as evidence that merge\n      amplification is \"task-general\" and not an artifact of NER-specific evaluation.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Empirical confusability from 5-10 calibration examples, no gradients. Compare vs SDGD-zero.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sdgd_cal_f1: 0.30\n      sdgd_cal_precision: 0.24\n      sdgd_cal_recall: 0.40\n      sdgd_zero_f1: 0.2514\n      single_pass_f1: 0.4451\n      sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta: +0.05\n      sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta: -0.145\n      calibration_cost_examples: 8\n      calibration_cost_api_calls: 8\n      empirical_vs_embedding_confusion_rank_corr: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      Calibration improves confusability estimation (empirical confusion matrix is more\n      accurate than embedding cosine similarity — rank corr ≈0.38 vs perfect). SDGD-cal\n      creates better batch partitions than SDGD-zero: fewer high-confusion pairs co-batched.\n      This yields +5pp F1 over SDGD-zero (better precision from fewer intra-batch confusions).\n      However, SDGD-cal still loses to single-pass by 14.5pp because merge amplification\n      is independent of batch quality — it occurs at the merge step regardless of how\n      well-separated the batches are. Recall still higher than single-pass (0.40 vs 0.37)\n      confirming decomposition's recall benefit, but precision gap (0.24 vs 0.56) dominates.\n      The calibration cost is minimal (8 API calls) but the gain doesn't overcome the\n      fundamental merge problem. In median tier, this is a marginal improvement that\n      confirms the bottleneck diagnosis rather than solving it.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero vs Single-pass\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"P\", \"R\", \"Batches\", \"Calibration\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.445, 0.563, 0.368, 1, \"N/A\"]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.251, 0.210, 0.314, 8, \"None\"]\n          - [\"SDGD-cal\", 0.300, 0.240, 0.400, 8, \"8 examples\"]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.320, 0.249, 0.446, 8, \"N/A\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Confusion matrix heatmap: empirical vs embedding-predicted, showing calibration improves type-pair confusion ranking\"\n      - \"Precision-recall scatter: single-pass vs SDGD variants, showing all decompositions in high-recall low-precision quadrant\"\n    analysis: >\n      SDGD-cal demonstrates that better confusability estimation helps batch composition\n      (+5pp over SDGD-zero) but cannot overcome merge amplification. Notably, random-batch\n      still beats SDGD-cal in F1 (0.32 vs 0.30) because random batching doesn't systematically\n      concentrate difficult types — it achieves better precision through lucky mixing.\n      This is the smoking gun for \"the problem is merge, not partition.\" Paper frames\n      SDGD-cal as ablation evidence isolating the merge bottleneck.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD-cal on MAVEN\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"Empirical confusability with calibration on 168 event types. Full comparison table.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sdgd_cal_relaxed_f1: 0.19\n      sdgd_cal_precision: 0.14\n      sdgd_cal_recall: 0.32\n      sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1: 0.17\n      single_pass_relaxed_f1: 0.245\n      sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta: +0.02\n      sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta: -0.055\n      calibration_cost_examples: 10\n    result_rationale: >\n      On MAVEN, SDGD-cal's improvement over SDGD-zero is smaller (+2pp vs +5pp on Few-NERD)\n      because: (1) the semantically homogeneous schema means even empirical calibration\n      struggles to find clean partitions — most type pairs have non-trivial confusability,\n      (2) 168 types require 12+ batches, amplifying merge issues further, (3) event trigger\n      boundaries are inherently fuzzy, so merge deduplication errors compound. The gap\n      between SDGD-cal and single-pass (-5.5pp) is smaller than Few-NERD (-14.5pp) in\n      relative terms because MAVEN single-pass baseline is already lower (0.245 vs 0.445).\n      Pattern is consistent: calibration helps confusability estimation but merge amplification\n      remains the binding constraint.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: MAVEN Full Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Relaxed F1\", \"P\", \"R\", \"Batches\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.245, 0.28, 0.22, 1]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.17, 0.12, 0.29, 12]\n          - [\"SDGD-cal\", 0.19, 0.14, 0.32, 12]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.19, 0.14, 0.30, 12]\n          - [\"Per-type\", 0.15, 0.09, 0.41, 168]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-task merge amplification: Few-NERD vs MAVEN decomposition penalty, showing consistent pattern\"\n    analysis: >\n      MAVEN confirms merge amplification is task-general. SDGD-cal ≈ random-batch on MAVEN\n      (both 0.19), reinforcing that partition quality is secondary to merge quality.\n      Paper presents the full 2-task × 5-method table as Table 4, with merge amplification\n      as the unified explanation for all decomposition failures.\n\n  - name: \"LLaMA Multi-Model Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"Replicate exp-002 protocol on Few-NERD with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct. Addresses single-model concern.\"\n    expected_results:\n      pairwise_pearson_r: 0.08\n      pairwise_pearson_p: 0.0004\n      pairwise_n_pairs: 1953\n      pairwise_spearman_rho: 0.07\n      pairwise_spearman_p: 0.002\n      nonzero_spearman_rho: 0.15\n      nonzero_spearman_p: 0.012\n      per_type_spearman_rho: -0.18\n      per_type_p: 0.08\n      count_6_f1: 0.28\n      count_12_f1: 0.31\n      confusion_ratio_extreme_count_6: 4.2\n      confusion_ratio_extreme_count_12: 5.8\n      bcs_effect_count_12_delta_f1: -0.06\n      f1_vs_bcs_partial_r_count_12: 0.32\n      f1_vs_bcs_partial_p_count_12: 0.04\n    result_rationale: >\n      LLaMA-3.3-70B shows directionally consistent but weaker signal than Qwen3-32B because:\n      (1) LLaMA's instruction-following for structured NER output is less reliable — more\n      format errors and missed entities reduce statistical power by adding noise uncorrelated\n      with BCS, (2) LLaMA-70B has more capacity than Qwen3-32B, potentially better at\n      distinguishing similar types (higher baseline → smaller confusability effect), (3) the\n      Qwen3 embeddings used for BCS computation are naturally more predictive of Qwen3's\n      confusion patterns than LLaMA's — there's an embedding-model alignment effect\n      (documented in D010 for MiniLM vs Qwen3). Pairwise r=0.08 is significant (p<0.001,\n      n=1953) but half the Qwen3 effect (0.156). Per-type analysis trends negative (ρ=-0.18)\n      but p=0.08 misses significance — consistent with median tier definition \"directional\n      but not compelling standalone.\" Confusion ratio 4-6x is above 1 but well below Qwen3's\n      10-28x, because LLaMA's higher capacity means extreme batches still partially resolve\n      confusable types. The cross-model BCS effect delta is -6pp (vs Qwen3's implied ~-8pp\n      at count_12), falling in the defined range [-20, -5].\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Qwen3-32B\", \"LLaMA-3.3-70B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Pairwise r\", 0.156, 0.08]\n          - [\"Pairwise p\", \"4.6e-12\", \"4e-4\"]\n          - [\"Nonzero ρ\", 0.254, 0.15]\n          - [\"Per-type ρ\", -0.293, -0.18]\n          - [\"Per-type p\", 0.020, 0.08]\n          - [\"Confusion ratio (extreme)\", \"10-28x\", \"4-6x\"]\n      - name: \"Table: LLaMA Count Curve\"\n        columns: [\"count\", \"F1\", \"Precision\", \"Recall\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.30, 0.20, 0.58]\n          - [6, 0.28, 0.22, 0.39]\n          - [12, 0.31, 0.30, 0.32]\n          - [22, 0.27, 0.31, 0.24]\n          - [44, 0.35, 0.40, 0.31]\n          - [64, 0.33, 0.45, 0.26]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side pairwise SCS vs confusion scatter: Qwen3 (left) with clear trend, LLaMA (right) with noisier but directional trend\"\n      - \"Cross-model count curves overlaid, showing parallel degradation patterns\"\n    analysis: >\n      LLaMA replicates direction on all metrics but with roughly half the effect size.\n      Pairwise signal remains significant (p<0.001) — sufficient for \"consistent direction\n      across models.\" Per-type p=0.08 means we cannot claim per-type significance on LLaMA\n      alone, but combined with Qwen3 the pattern is clear. Paper softens multi-model claim\n      to \"consistent direction across models, strongest on primary model (Qwen3-32B)\" and\n      notes embedding-model alignment as a factor. This is adequate for EMNLP Main with\n      the softened claim; a reviewer requesting LLaMA-specific embeddings for BCS is\n      addressable in rebuttal but not pre-submission given timeline.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation: Random Batching\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: \"Random batching with same k as SDGD. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\"\n    expected_results:\n      random_k8_f1: 0.315\n      random_k8_precision: 0.245\n      random_k8_recall: 0.44\n      random_k8_std_f1: 0.025\n      sdgd_zero_k8_f1: 0.251\n      single_pass_f1: 0.445\n      random_vs_sdgd_delta: +0.064\n      random_vs_single_delta: -0.130\n      n_random_seeds: 10\n      random_seed_f1_range: [0.28, 0.35]\n    result_rationale: >\n      Random batching at k=8 outperforms SDGD-zero (+6.4pp F1), replicating exp-008's\n      finding. This occurs because SDGD-zero's confusability-minimizing partition\n      concentrates semantically distant types together — these types often span different\n      domains (e.g., person subtypes + location subtypes), and the model performs worse\n      when batch types are too heterogeneous (loses cross-type contextual priming).\n      Random batches accidentally achieve better balance between similarity separation\n      and contextual coherence. However, random batching still loses to single-pass\n      by 13pp because merge amplification affects all decomposition equally. The 10-seed\n      variance (σ=0.025) shows random batching is moderately stable. In median tier,\n      this ablation is the key evidence that batch composition quality is NOT the bottleneck —\n      merge strategy is.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Random Batching Ablation (10 seeds)\"\n        columns: [\"Seed\", \"F1\", \"P\", \"R\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"mean\", 0.315, 0.245, 0.44]\n          - [\"std\", 0.025, 0.02, 0.03]\n          - [\"min\", 0.28, 0.21, 0.39]\n          - [\"max\", 0.35, 0.28, 0.49]\n      - name: \"Table: Decomposition Method Ranking (Few-NERD)\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"ΔF1 vs single\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.445, \"—\"]\n          - [\"Random-batch (k=8)\", 0.315, -0.130]\n          - [\"Per-type (k=66)\", 0.292, -0.153]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero (k=8)\", 0.251, -0.194]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Box plot: F1 distribution across 10 random seeds vs SDGD-zero point estimate\"\n      - \"Merge amplification curve: duplicate prediction rate vs number of batches\"\n    analysis: >\n      Random > SDGD-zero is paradoxical if you assume confusability-aware partition should\n      help. Paper explains: SDGD-zero optimizes for one objective (minimize intra-batch\n      confusability) but ignores another (maintain contextual coherence). The real\n      bottleneck is post-merge deduplication, which degrades proportionally to batch count\n      regardless of batch composition. This motivates framing SDGD's failure as revealing\n      merge amplification rather than as a partition failure.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation: Per-Type Splitting\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: \"UniNER-style 1 type per batch. Upper bound on decomposition recall, reveals latency-quality tradeoff.\"\n    expected_results:\n      per_type_f1: 0.292\n      per_type_precision: 0.194\n      per_type_recall: 0.586\n      per_type_batches: 66\n      per_type_time_s: 2532\n      per_type_recall_vs_single: +0.218\n      per_type_precision_vs_single: -0.369\n      per_type_f1_vs_single: -0.153\n      per_type_unique_preds: 8450\n      single_pass_unique_preds: 3200\n      merge_dup_rate: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      Per-type splitting eliminates ALL type confusion (zero intra-batch confusability)\n      and maximizes recall (0.586 vs single-pass 0.368, +21.8pp). But precision collapses\n      (0.194 vs 0.563, -36.9pp) because: (1) each batch extracts aggressively for its\n      one type — without competing types, the model over-predicts, (2) merging 66 batch\n      outputs creates massive duplication — the same span extracted as different types in\n      different batches, (3) merge heuristics (longest match, confidence-based) cannot\n      reliably disambiguate since each batch's prediction is locally confident. The 38%\n      merge duplicate rate quantifies this directly. Per-type is the theoretical upper\n      bound for recall under decomposition but the worst-case for precision. The 33x\n      latency overhead (2532s vs 77s) makes it impractical regardless. This is already\n      in exp-008 metrics; exp-014 adds systematic analysis of WHY precision drops (merge\n      duplication rate, over-prediction per batch).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Per-Type Detailed Breakdown\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Single-pass\", \"Per-type\", \"Delta\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"F1\", 0.445, 0.292, -0.153]\n          - [\"Precision\", 0.563, 0.194, -0.369]\n          - [\"Recall\", 0.368, 0.586, +0.218]\n          - [\"Unique preds\", 3200, 8450, \"+164%\"]\n          - [\"Merge duplicates\", \"N/A\", \"38%\", \"\"]\n          - [\"Time (s)\", 77, 2532, \"33x\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Precision-recall frontier: single-pass, random-k, SDGD-zero, per-type — showing decomposition Pareto frontier below single-pass\"\n      - \"Merge duplicate rate vs batch count, showing approximately linear scaling\"\n    analysis: >\n      Per-type confirms the merge amplification mechanism: recall scales with decomposition\n      granularity but precision degrades faster. The Pareto frontier shows no decomposition\n      point dominates single-pass on F1. This is the strongest evidence for \"merge\n      amplification as fundamental bottleneck\" — even the oracle partition (zero confusability)\n      cannot beat single-pass without a better merge strategy. Paper uses per-type as\n      the upper bound in the decomposition analysis table.\n\n  - name: \"SCS Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: \"Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix. Validates embedding proxy.\"\n    expected_results:\n      spearman_rho_qwen3: 0.35\n      spearman_p_qwen3: 0.0001\n      kendall_tau_qwen3: 0.27\n      spearman_rho_minilm: 0.28\n      spearman_p_minilm: 0.001\n      top10_overlap_qwen3: 0.5\n      top10_overlap_minilm: 0.4\n      n_pairs_with_empirical: 420\n    result_rationale: >\n      Embedding SCS is a moderate proxy for empirical confusion (ρ=0.35 for Qwen3, 0.28\n      for MiniLM). The correlation is significant but far from perfect because: (1) embedding\n      similarity captures semantic relatedness, not confusion — \"person-artist\" and\n      \"person-athlete\" are semantically similar but the model may distinguish them well\n      from context, (2) empirical confusion is asymmetric (model confuses A→B more than\n      B→A) while cosine similarity is symmetric, (3) empirical confusion is heavily\n      influenced by type frequency and context patterns not captured by type-name embeddings.\n      Qwen3 embeddings outperform MiniLM (ρ=0.35 vs 0.28) on Few-NERD because of\n      extraction-model alignment (consistent with D010 findings). Top-10 overlap at 50%\n      means half the most-confused empirical pairs are also highest-SCS — useful but not\n      sufficient for precision. This validates SCS as a reasonable zero-shot proxy while\n      motivating calibration-based approaches (SDGD-cal). In median tier, this is\n      adequate evidence for \"embedding SCS is a useful but imperfect confusability proxy.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SCS vs Empirical Confusion Rank Correlation\"\n        columns: [\"Embedding\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"p\", \"Kendall τ\", \"Top-10 overlap\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-32B\", 0.35, \"1e-4\", 0.27, \"5/10\"]\n          - [\"MiniLM-L6-v2\", 0.28, \"1e-3\", 0.21, \"4/10\"]\n      - name: \"Table: Top Confused Pairs — Empirical vs Predicted\"\n        columns: [\"Rank\", \"Empirical top pair\", \"Emp. confusion\", \"SCS rank\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, \"person-other ↔ person-politician\", 156, 2]\n          - [2, \"person-other ↔ person-artist\", 98, 5]\n          - [3, \"person-athlete ↔ person-other\", 87, 3]\n          - [4, \"location-GPE ↔ location-bodiesofwater\", 45, 18]\n          - [5, \"building-library ↔ building-other\", 41, 7]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter: embedding SCS (x) vs empirical confusion count (y), with top-10 pairs labeled\"\n      - \"Rank comparison heatmap: top-20 empirical vs top-20 SCS pairs, showing overlap region\"\n    analysis: >\n      ρ=0.35 validates SCS as a directionally useful proxy — significantly better than\n      random (p<0.001) but insufficient for precise partition optimization. The asymmetry\n      insight (top empirical pair person-other↔politician at SCS rank 2, but location-GPE↔\n      bodiesofwater at SCS rank 18) suggests type-name semantics capture intra-domain\n      confusion well but miss cross-domain false positives. Paper presents this as\n      motivation for calibration-based approaches and as explaining why SDGD-zero\n      underperforms SDGD-cal. Also explains the D010 embedding sensitivity finding —\n      different embeddings capture different aspects of confusability.\n\n  - name: \"SDGD v2 on Few-NERD\"\n    plan_id: \"exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd\"\n    description: \"SDGD v2 with merge amplification mitigation (confidence thresholding, duplicate suppression). Tests whether fixing merge recovers SDGD benefit.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sdgd_v2_f1: 0.42\n      sdgd_v2_precision: 0.44\n      sdgd_v2_recall: 0.40\n      sdgd_v2_vs_single_delta_f1: -0.025\n      sdgd_v2_vs_zero_delta_f1: +0.169\n      sdgd_v2_precision_drop_vs_single: -0.123\n      merge_dup_rate_v2: 0.12\n      merge_dup_rate_v1: 0.38\n      confidence_threshold: 0.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      SDGD v2 partially mitigates merge amplification through confidence-based deduplication\n      and span-level voting across batches. Merge duplicate rate drops from 38% to 12%.\n      Precision recovers substantially (0.44 vs SDGD-zero's 0.21) but still below single-pass\n      (0.56) because: (1) confidence calibration is imperfect — the model's confidence\n      doesn't reliably distinguish correct from incorrect type assignments, (2) some\n      legitimate multi-type spans get suppressed by dedup, reducing recall slightly,\n      (3) without explicit negative examples per batch, the model still over-extracts\n      for the types it sees. Net F1 is 0.42, within -2.5pp of single-pass — a massive\n      improvement over SDGD-zero but not a clear win. In median tier, this is \"marginal\n      or negative\" — the delta is within noise for a method that costs 5x more API calls.\n      Paper frames as: \"merge mitigation partially recovers decomposition benefit but does\n      not achieve the positive result needed to claim SDGD as a practical method.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SDGD v2 vs All Methods\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"F1\", \"P\", \"R\", \"Merge dup%\", \"Time(s)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass\", 0.445, 0.563, 0.368, \"N/A\", 77]\n          - [\"SDGD-zero\", 0.251, 0.210, 0.314, \"38%\", 409]\n          - [\"SDGD v2\", 0.420, 0.440, 0.400, \"12%\", 450]\n          - [\"Random-batch\", 0.320, 0.249, 0.446, \"35%\", 476]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Before/after merge mitigation: precision-recall shift from SDGD-zero to v2, approaching but not reaching single-pass\"\n      - \"Merge duplicate rate: v1 vs v2 across batch counts, showing v2 mitigation effectiveness\"\n    analysis: >\n      SDGD v2 demonstrates that merge amplification IS the bottleneck (fixing it recovers\n      most of the F1 gap) but the fix is incomplete. F1 delta of -2.5pp vs single-pass\n      means SDGD v2 is not cost-effective (5x latency for -2.5pp). Paper pivots: SDGD's\n      primary contribution is diagnostic (merge amplification) rather than practical\n      (improved extraction). The v2 result becomes evidence for \"merge amplification is\n      partially addressable but fundamentally limits naive decomposition approaches.\"\n      This explains why UniNER and LLM-PEE report modest, inconsistent gains — they\n      also face merge amplification but don't characterize it.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"84d9748a-c232-409c-a5b7-d28c90471d97","total_cost_usd":0.42037675,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20497,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":10831,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20497,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10831,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20497,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20497},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":15450,"outputTokens":23,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.015565,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":10831,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":20497,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.40481175,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"4bd7ac11-488f-4065-8ae0-cac35d435ed2"}
